1
00:00:49,565 --> 00:00:55,565
Amerigo Tot is a drama film directed by Zoltán Huszárik.

2
00:01:39,130 --> 00:01:45,130
A short film on Hungarian-born artist and sculptor Amerigo Tot.

3
00:02:28,695 --> 00:02:34,695
Himslef, played by Amerigo Tot.

4
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:24,260
Amerigo Tot (born Imre Tóth; 27 September 1909 – 13 December
1984) was a Hungarian sculptor and occasional actor.

5
00:04:07,826 --> 00:04:13,826
Born in Fehérvárcsurgó, Austria-Hungary he moved to Rome towards the end
of the 1920s, where he lived for the rest of his life.

6
00:04:57,391 --> 00:05:03,391
He studied in Budapest under Ferenc Helbing and György Leszkovszky from
1926 until 1928, and then at the Bauhaus in Germany.

7
00:05:46,956 --> 00:05:52,956
As the Nazis came to power he moved to Rome where he worked sculpting memorials on a grant
from the Roman-Hungarian Academy, where he eventually became an advisor.

8
00:06:36,521 --> 00:06:42,521
He fought in the Italian resistance movement starting in 1943.

9
00:07:26,086 --> 00:07:32,086
He first received international recognition for his work on
the frieze in Roma Termini station in 1950.

10
00:08:15,652 --> 00:08:21,652
He began doing abstract works in the 1950s.

11
00:09:05,217 --> 00:09:11,217
He returned home to Hungary several times, including 1937, 1939 and 1969 in what was
a carefully prepared trip by the Hungarian communist culture-buro.

12
00:09:54,782 --> 00:10:00,782
In Hungary he was celebrated as a "world famous" artist and had big exhibitions.

13
00:10:44,347 --> 00:10:50,347
He did traditional works, including a Madonna sculpture in his native Fehérvárcsurgó, as well as abstract sculptures and public
monuments, including Microcosm in Macrocosm (a tribute to Béla Bartók), His Majesty and The Kilowatt in Kecskemét.

14
00:11:33,913 --> 00:11:39,913
The Amerigo Tot Museum in Budapest is named after him.

15
00:12:23,478 --> 00:12:29,478
In the 1960s and 1970s he made small appearances in films.

16
00:13:13,043 --> 00:13:19,043
He is perhaps best-known to English-speaking audiences for his role as Bussetta, Michael
Corleone's bodyguard and executioner of Johnny Ola in The Godfather Part II.

17
00:14:02,608 --> 00:14:08,608
According to George S.

18
00:14:52,173 --> 00:14:58,173
Larke-Walsh, Tot's character in the film had to be brutal
so that Michael's authority was retained.

19
00:15:41,739 --> 00:15:47,739
Tot also appeared in The Most Beautiful Wife and Pulp (1972).

20
00:16:31,304 --> 00:16:37,304
The latter was directed by Mike Hodges for United
Artists and featured Tot as Sotgio.

21
00:17:20,869 --> 00:17:26,869
Tot died in Rome in 1984.

22
00:18:10,434 --> 00:18:16,434
He is interred in the Farkasréti Cemetery in Budapest.
